00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.920
- [Instructor] We're told
the graph of y is equal

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:04.980
to square root of x is
shown below, fair enough.

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:06.560
Which of the following is the graph

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:10.360
of y is equal to two times the square root

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:13.360
of negative x minus one?

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:15.980
And they give us some choices here,

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:19.140
and so I encourage you to pause this video

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:20.440
and try to figure it out on your own

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:22.423
before we work through this together.

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:24.690
All right, now let's work
through this together,

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:25.700
and the way that I'm going to do it

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:27.430
is I'm actually going to try to draw

00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:29.200
what the graph of two
times the square root

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:31.370
of negative x minus one should look like,

00:00:31.370 --> 00:00:33.300
and then I'll just look
at which of the choices

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:34.990
is closest to what I drew.

00:00:34.990 --> 00:00:36.270
And the way that I'm going to do that

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:37.740
is I'm going to do it step by step,

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:41.260
so we already see what
y equals the square root

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:42.900
of x looks like,

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:44.930
but let's say we just want to build up.

00:00:44.930 --> 00:00:47.040
So let's say we want to now figure out

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:50.160
what is the graph of y is
equal to the square root of?

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:52.170
Instead of an x under the radical sign,

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:55.210
let me put a negative x
under the radical sign.

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:56.980
What would that do to it?

00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:59.870
Well, whatever was happening
at a certain value of x

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:02.540
will now happen at the
negative of that value of x.

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:05.220
So the square root of x is not
defined for negative numbers.

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:08.100
Now this one won't be
defined for positive numbers.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.690
And the behavior that
you saw at x equals two,

00:01:10.690 --> 00:01:13.710
you would now see at
x equals negative two.

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:15.470
The behavior that you
saw at x equals four,

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:18.660
you will now see at x
equals negative four,

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:21.110
and so on and so forth.

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:24.350
So the y equals the
square root of negative x

00:01:25.327 --> 00:01:26.970
is going to look like this.

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:30.200
You've essentially flipped it over the y.

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:32.770
We have flipped it over the y axis.

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:35.090
All right, so we've done this part.

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:36.270
Now let's scale that.

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:38.820
Now let's multiply that by two.

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:41.840
So what would y is equal to
two times the square root

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:43.453
of negative x look like?

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:45.950
Well, it would look like this red curve,

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:47.140
but at any given x value,

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:49.220
we're gonna get twice as high.

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:51.480
So at x equals negative four,

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:53.210
instead of getting to two,

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:54.810
we're now going to get to four.

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:56.520
At x equals negative nine,

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:58.210
instead of getting to three,

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:00.240
we are now going to get to six.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:02.340
Now at x equals zero, we're
still going to be at zero

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:03.940
'cause two times zero is zero,

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:05.420
so it's going to look,

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:08.040
it's going to look like that.

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:09.980
Something like that,

00:02:09.980 --> 00:02:12.890
so that's y equals two times
the square root of negative x.

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:15.300
And then last but not least,

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:17.630
what will y,

00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:19.430
let me do that in a different color.

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:23.810
What will y equals two
times the square root

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:27.030
of negative x minus one look like?

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:28.930
Well, whatever y value
we were getting before,

00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:32.760
we're now just going to
shift everything down by one.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:34.020
So if we were at six before,

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:35.613
we're going to be at five now.

00:02:36.989 --> 00:02:38.070
If we were at four before,

00:02:38.070 --> 00:02:39.980
we're now going to be at three.

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:41.570
If we were at zero before,

00:02:41.570 --> 00:02:44.140
we're now going to be at negative one,

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:48.390
and so our curve is going
to look something like,

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:50.500
something like that.

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:54.920
So let's look for, let's see
which choices match that.

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:59.000
So let me scroll down here,

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:03.320
and both C and D kind of
look right, but notice,

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:05.740
right at zero, we want
it to be at negative one,

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:10.347
so D is exactly what we had drawn.

00:03:10.347 --> 00:03:11.990
And at nine, we're at five.

00:03:11.990 --> 00:03:14.100
Or at negative nine, we're at five.

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:15.730
At negative four, we're at three,

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:17.010
and at zero, we're at negative one.

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:18.720
Exactly what we had drawn.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:19.973
Let's do another example.

